{"body":"## Goal

Prove or reject one bounded, task-specific 0.5B path for cnm's bundled offline commit-message generator after unmodified 1B and 2B models failed. This pierce tests whether specialization plus deterministic built-in-style rendering can preserve the complete message contract in a genuinely smaller distribution.

## Maintainer decision

On 2026-07-25 the maintainer rejected an approximately 4 GB bundled model and confirmed:

- train one approximately 0.5B model specifically for commit-message generation;
- render only the mechanical parts of built-in styles in code;
- keep `auto`, `conventional`, `angular`, `google`, `atom`, `plain`, and `custom`;
- keep additive Custom Guidance, primary `custom` guidance, and multi-line messages;
- keep normal generation fully offline with no API, Ollama, or first-run download;
- cap the complete installed distribution at **700,000,000 bytes** per platform;
- do not begin the Git workflow rewrite unless this pierce passes.

This changes the internal responsibility split, not the user-visible message contract.

## Frozen base family

- Base: `Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-0.5B-Instruct`
- Official revision: `ea3f2471cf1b1f0db85067f1ef93848e38e88c25`
- Parameters: 494,032,768 total / approximately 0.36B non-embedding
- Architecture: Qwen2 causal LM, code-specialized, instruction-tuned
- Context: 32,768 tokens
- License: Apache-2.0
- Training representation: BF16 MLX conversion from the official revision; source weights, tokenizer, template, and conversion hashes must be preserved
- Distribution target: fused GGUF, Q5_K_M first
- Runtime: one pinned, stripped llama.cpp build

Size planning reference only:

- GGUF repo: `Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-0.5B-Instruct-GGUF`
- Revision: `ebb2015119c907b064c512bf053e945850b5875f`
- Q5_K_M bytes: `522,186,624`
- Q5_K_M SHA-256: `7f6a0b1670c9fff2cccbf8746dff19bb9c023708c8ca7702e4d49805985ecbac`

This reference conversion is not the final trained artifact. No second base-model family is allowed. If the two allowed training configurations fail, stop.

## Frozen responsibility boundary

The model emits one grammar-constrained semantic record:

- `type`: a valid Conventional/Angular type or null;
- `scope`: a concise scope or null;
- `subject`: the complete semantic subject content;
- `body`: the complete optional body content.

The renderer API is exactly `render(resolvedStyle, semanticRecord)`:

- it may add only the fixed prefix/separator required by `resolvedStyle` and join subject/body with one blank line;
- it must not read the diff, Guidance, history, expected output, case ID, or gold metadata;
- it must not add, delete, translate, lowercase, capitalize, paraphrase, or otherwise repair `subject` or `body`;
- Conventional/Angular require a valid model-produced `type`; missing/invalid type rejects instead of defaulting;
- optional scope may only be emitted or omitted from the model record, never inferred by the renderer;
- custom subject/body content passes through unchanged apart from universal parse/shape validation.

`auto` history resolution is a separate deterministic function that may read only recent commit messages. It produces `resolvedStyle`; it cannot read the current diff or Guidance. The model may receive history as style context, but copied history replacing current-diff semantics is a failure.

Score both the raw semantic record and final rendered message. Renderer syntax success cannot turn a semantically wrong or Guidance-noncompliant record into a pass.

Before model evaluation, mutation self-tests must prove that swapped subjects, deleted bodies, invalid type/scope, copied history, ignored Guidance, malformed JSON, Markdown, and explanation text remain rejected or semantically failing.

## Frozen data source and acceptance

Primary source only:

- Dataset: `JetBrains-Research/commit-chronicle`
- Revision: `5fd076e67b812a9f3d1999e5e40f71715f84bb51`
- Shape: one commit row with complete `mods[]`, original message, repository, SHA, language, and source-repository license
- Allowlisted source licenses: MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD-3-Clause

Explicitly excluded: CommitPack/CommitPackFT single-file slices and CommitBench's CC BY-NC dataset.

Freeze the final corpus before any base or trained-model output is inspected. Initial target is 8,000 complete commit families, approximately 6,000/1,000/1,000 train/validation/test, with these predeclared requirements:

- split by canonical upstream/fork group; repository sets must be disjoint;
- `(repo, commit)`, exact normalized patch, path-independent changed-line shingles, and target-message overlap checked across all train/validation/test/shadow/historical sets;
- every retained row aggregates all textual `mods`; any missing/binary/unknown modification drops the entire commit; no diff is truncated;
- at least 60% of families change 2–8 files; report 1/2–3/4–8 file bins and tokenizer-length bins;
- report language, license, change type, body, style, Guidance-intent, repository, file-count, and token-length distributions;
- source message is a candidate label, not unquestioned truth: clean trailers/URLs but preserve grounded subject/body; derive type/scope only when the source message supports them;
- automated grounding, secret/credential/high-entropy, PII, malformed patch, trivial message, merge/revert/bot, exact and near-duplicate checks run on every row;
- the secret scan covers diff, message, history, generated transformations, and manifests before any external processing;
- deterministic transformations cover all styles and Guidance intents, while intent families and paraphrase templates are isolated across train/validation/shadow;
- no private project diff is sent to an external labeling service.

Use a fixed seed to blind-audit 200 retained rows against their complete diff for completeness, groundedness, label correctness, secrets/PII, and provenance. Pass only with zero critical error and at least 95% fully grounded labels; otherwise fix the source/filter design and resample from a newly frozen seed before training.

The old #31 corpus may contribute reviewed anchors only. Its 840 direct-output records and single-file CommitPackFT families are not accepted as the primary corpus.

## Independent evaluation isolation

The 26 cases used by #31–#33 are now a **public regression set**, not final GO evidence. Their raw cases and failure modes are known.

Before selecting training rows:

1. An independent reviewer freezes an unread shadow gate from real, complete, multi-file commits after the base-model revision date (2024-11-18), using canonical repositories excluded from training.
2. The training operator receives only the shadow bundle hash, rubric, coverage matrix, repository exclusion groups, and case count—not raw diffs, gold records, or Guidance wording.
3. The shadow gate contains at least 30 cases across all seven styles, at least 10 unseen/paraphrased Guidance cases, at least 6 required-body cases, composition cases such as Chinese+Conventional and prefix+body, a near-limit complete diff, and an over-limit reject case.
4. A 50–100 commit historical set, canonical repo groups, sample algorithm, commit IDs hash, and rubric are also frozen before training. Public pre-cutoff commits may be regression evidence but not the only generalization evidence.
5. Both training configurations and checkpoint selection rules are frozen before any base/regression/shadow model output is inspected.
6. Train/validation alone select the final BF16 checkpoint. Public regression output is diagnostic only and cannot select or modify a configuration.
7. The selected configuration is cleanly retrained once from an empty run directory before the one-time shadow gate. Reproducibility means the same selected checkpoint rule and metrics within predeclared tolerance, not cross-device bit identity.
8. If any shadow output is used to alter training, decoding, prompt, grammar, parser, renderer, or configuration, that shadow gate is retired and a new independent unseen gate is required.

## Frozen decode and evaluator

Freeze before base baseline:

- official chat template hash;
- complete prompt and semantic JSON grammar;
- parser, `auto` resolver, renderer, and mutation self-tests;
- greedy decoding (`temperature=0`, no sampling), `top_p=1`, `top_k=0`, `repeat_penalty=1`, seed `424242`;
- maximum 256 generated tokens;
- EOS/stop behavior and 32,768 total context;
- thread/GPU/batch settings for BF16 and llama.cpp;
- tokenizer-based total token formula reserving prompt, history, Guidance, grammar output, and generation budget;
- an explicit maximum Commit Input; over-limit input must reject before inference and never truncate silently.

Exact commands, lockfiles, OS/framework versions, data order, RNG state, checkpoints, conversion revision, llama.cpp revision, and hashes must persist under `.cs/epics/001-o-offline-commit-flow/artifacts/034/`. Raw licensed datasets and large weights stay outside Git but have content-addressed hashes and reproducible acquisition commands.

## Hardware gate

Current host:

- Apple M5 Pro, 18 CPU cores / 20 GPU cores;
- 48 GB unified memory;
- MLX `max_recommended_working_set_size`: 40,200,896,512 bytes.

#31 reference only: Gemma 270M LoRA rank 8 at max sequence 10,240 used a reported 19.971 GB and ran 1,200 iterations in 222 seconds. That metric is not treated as directly comparable to RSS or total unified-memory pressure.

For each predeclared training configuration, run a smoke with the exact dtype, sequence cap, microbatch, gradient accumulation, target layers, checkpointing, optimizer, forward/backward/update path, one validation pass, and one checkpoint save.

Record only whitelisted hardware fields. Never persist hostname, username, serial number, hardware UUID, provisioning UDID, or activation-lock data.

Measure:

- `/usr/bin/time -l` maximum RSS;
- MLX active, cache, and peak Metal allocation;
- `memory_pressure -Q` throughout;
- `vm_stat` pageout/compression/swap counters before and after;
- median step time and projected full-run wall time.

Continue on this Mac only when:

- no OOM or non-finite loss occurs;
- MLX peak allocation stays below 80% of the reported recommended working set;
- memory pressure remains normal with no run-attributable pageout or swapout;
- median smoke step time is at most 2 seconds;
- each projected configuration completes within 6 hours.

If this gate fails, stop before full training and prepare a reproducible RTX 5080 / WSL2 handoff. The handoff must pin driver, CUDA, PyTorch, training framework, attention implementation, VRAM/offload policy, and rerun the equivalent smoke; it is not permission to build a general cross-platform training system.

## Training budget

- At most two predeclared LoRA/SFT configurations; no open-ended search.
- Use assistant-output-only loss.
- Select checkpoints only by frozen validation metrics.
- Evaluate BF16+adapter, then cleanly retrain the selected configuration.
- No full-parameter training, DPO/RL, model merge, RAG, ensemble, fallback model, or provider abstraction.

## Risk order and stop conditions

### 1. Independent gate, data, renderer, and reproducibility freeze

Shadow/historical hashes, exclusions, acceptance manifest, evaluator, configs, decode, artifacts, and mutation tests must exist before model output. If they cannot be frozen without leakage, stop.

### 2. Pre-training 700 MB skeleton

Build the intended macOS installed layout using the pinned Q5 reference, stripped runtime and transitive non-system libraries, launcher/parser/renderer stub, license, and notices. Count logical installed bytes, including duplicated wrapper/package files. Stop before training if the skeleton exceeds 700,000,000 bytes or leaves no documented growth margin. Repeat with the actual fused Q5 after quality passes.

### 3. Data validity and audit

Apply the corpus acceptance above. Stop if a trustworthy full-commit corpus cannot be assembled within scope.

### 4. Hardware smoke

Apply the per-configuration Mac gate. Mac failure triggers handoff, not a model-quality conclusion.

### 5. Base baseline and public regression

After all freezes, run the unmodified BF16 base through the complete record/renderer path. Record raw and rendered results for comparison. It does not consume a training configuration and cannot change either frozen configuration.

### 6. Training and validation selection

Run both frozen configurations unless an objective optimization failure invalidates one. Select and cleanly retrain using train/validation only. Do not inspect shadow data.

### 7. One-time BF16 shadow quality

Independent blinded scoring remains 0/1/2:

- `2`: accurate and sufficiently complete, with all requested Guidance and no invented material behavior;
- `1`: relevant but incomplete or imprecise;
- `0`: wrong, empty, malformed, code/diff echo, materially invented, or unusable.

Pass only when:

- no shadow case scores `0`;
- at least 28/30 score `2` (scale proportionally if the frozen gate contains more than 30 cases);
- all explicit Guidance and required-body cases pass exactly;
- all Conventional/Angular final formats pass;
- raw semantic records and final messages contain no Markdown, explanation, repetition, history substitution, or code/diff echo;
- the near-limit case is semantically correct and the over-limit case rejects before inference.

The public 26-case regression should still meet its old 24/26 full, no-0, Guidance 5/5, body 4/4 thresholds, but cannot independently produce GO.

If BF16 fails, **STOP**. Do not quantize, package the final runtime, or implement Git writes.

### 8. Conversion parity and Q5 shadow quality

Before quantization, fuse the adapter and run a small F16/BF16 GGUF parity set through the pinned llama.cpp to isolate tokenizer/template/conversion errors. Only after parity passes, quantize to Q5_K_M and rerun the unchanged shadow and regression gates. Q5 must meet every BF16 threshold; renderer success cannot hide quantization regression.

### 9. Historical, complete size, performance, and offline proof

Only after Q5 passes:

- independently score the frozen 50–100 historical set; `usable` means score `2`, at least 90% must be usable, and any score `0` or critical hallucination fails;
- measure the actual complete installed tree and require `<= 700,000,000` bytes;
- record cold/warm latency, prompt/decode throughput, and peak RSS on M5 Pro;
- ask the maintainer to accept explicit cold/warm/RSS thresholds before calling runtime performance GO;
- block outbound network access and rerun representative small, near-limit, style, Guidance, and multi-line cases.

## Quality commitments

- **Functional suitability:** unseen final rendered messages must pass semantic, style, Guidance, body, near-limit, over-limit, and historical gates.
- **Performance efficiency:** training hardware is gated before full runs; a pre-training skeleton and final installed tree enforce 700 MB; runtime measurements need maintainer acceptance.
- **Reliability:** independent holdouts, repository isolation, fixed configs/decode, clean retraining, exact manifests, conversion parity, and fresh-process reruns make the conclusion auditable.
- **Security:** normal generation is fully offline; secrets/PII are excluded before processing; hardware evidence is identity-sanitized.
- **Maintainability:** one model, one grammar, one renderer, one resolver, and one runtime; no platform, registry, or provider layer.

## Out of scope

- Git staging, commit execution, or repository mutation;
- production CLI/TUI implementation;
- changing the seven styles, Custom Guidance, or multi-line contract;
- arbitrary model search or a second base family;
- full-parameter training, preference optimization, RAG, ensembles, or fallback models;
- runtime model selection, downloaders, external API/Ollama fallback;
- cross-platform release work before quality and size pass;
- rewriting old product code or documentation beyond Epic status.

## Deliverables

- exact base/conversion/runtime/tool/license manifest and hashes;
- frozen shadow/historical manifests and exclusion groups without exposing raw gates to training;
- audited, licensed, leakage-checked data manifest and split hashes;
- frozen grammar, parser, resolver, renderer, evaluator, mutation tests, decode, token budget, and corpus hashes;
- pre-training size skeleton;
- sanitized Mac hardware smoke evidence or reproducible RTX 5080 / WSL2 handoff;
- two frozen configs, logs, checkpoints, clean-retrain comparison, and selection evidence;
- base, regression, one-time shadow, conversion parity, and—only after BF16 passes—Q5 evidence;
- if quality passes: historical, final size, runtime, maintainer performance acceptance, and offline evidence;
- explicit GO or STOP conclusion and Epic writeback.
